namespace Dakota {

/** \page StudyRuntimeDesign StudyRuntime Design Intent

\section StudyRuntimePurpose Purpose

StudyRuntime is the top-level execution-facing abstraction for a
constructed Dakota study graph. Its job is to give callers a single
runtime object they can use to execute an already-constructed top-level
Iterator in library mode, while keeping runtime concerns separate from
component semantics.

The key idea is that study construction and study execution are distinct
concerns. Models and iterators describe what the study means. StudyRuntime
describes how that already-constructed study is executed with a particular
ParallelLibrary and OutputManager.

\section StudyRuntimeResponsibilities Responsibilities

StudyRuntime has two roles.

First, it is the direct execution entry point for top-level API use. A
library-mode caller can construct semantic Dakota objects and then ask a
StudyRuntime to initialize communicators, execute the top-level iterator,
and tear communicators down.

Second, it acts as the runtime-side factory for IteratorContext objects.
That gives subordinate-execution clients such as NestedModel and the
meta-iterators a way to obtain execution helpers tied to the same runtime
services as the rest of the study.

\section StudyRuntimeNotPurpose What It Is Not

StudyRuntime is not intended to hold the semantic definition of a study.
It does not define variable mappings, response mappings, nested-study
meaning, or optimization/UQ algorithm behavior.

It is also not currently the main home of the low-level execution
mechanics. Much of the concrete MPI-aware iterator execution logic still
lives in IteratorExecutor and in existing iterator/model lifecycle code.
Today StudyRuntime is more facade than engine.

\section StudyRuntimeStable Stable Intent

The following ideas are intended to be relatively stable.

<ul>
<li>Top-level execution should be expressed as a runtime operation, not as an
intrinsic semantic operation of an Iterator or Model.</li>
<li>StudyRuntime should be the public-ish runtime handle for library-mode
execution of a constructed study graph.</li>
<li>StudyRuntime should define the boundary between semantic study objects and
runtime execution concerns.</li>
</ul>

\section StudyRuntimeExpectedChange Expected Evolution

The implementation under this abstraction is expected to evolve.

Today StudyRuntime is intentionally thin. Over time, more execution policy
may migrate behind this boundary. Examples include more unified handling of
top-level and subordinate execution, additional communicator lifecycle
coordination, and a cleaner home for execution policies that currently live
in callers or in IteratorExecutor.

In other words, the abstraction boundary is intended to remain, even though
the amount of behavior living behind it may grow.

\section StudyRuntimeRelationship Relationship To IteratorContext And IteratorExecutor

StudyRuntime is the runtime-level handle. IteratorContext is the
subordinate-execution helper created from that runtime for one specific
sub-iterator relationship. IteratorExecutor is the lower-level mechanism
that actually performs most of the iterator execution work.

A top-level API user generally interacts directly with StudyRuntime.
Dakota internals that need subordinate execution usually interact with an
IteratorContext, which in turn uses IteratorExecutor and sometimes routes
operations through StudyRuntime.

\section StudyRuntimeSeeAlso See Also

See \ref RuntimeExecutionDesign for the subsystem overview.

*/

} // namespace Dakota
